        "file_path": "/private/tmp/rp1-evals/fc305311/workspace/.rp1/work/features/yaml-validator/design-decisions.md",
        "content": "# Design Decisions: YAML Validator Command\n\n**Feature ID**: yaml-validator\n**Created**: 2026-01-25\n\n## Decision Log\n\n| ID | Decision | Choice | Rationale | Alternatives Considered |\n|----|----------|--------|-----------|------------------------|\n| D1 | YAML Parser Library | js-yaml (Bun built-in) | Bun runtime includes js-yaml, satisfying the no-new-dependencies constraint. Well-documented with good error reporting including line numbers. | None viable due to no-new-dependencies constraint |\n| D2 | Module Structure | Separate files (validator.ts, formatter.ts, types.ts) | Follows existing project pattern of modular separation. Enables isolated unit testing. Clear separation of concerns. | Single file implementation - rejected due to maintainability |\n| D3 | File Reading API | Bun.file().text() | Native Bun API for file reading. Returns Promise for async handling. Throws on file access errors with standard error codes. | Node fs.readFile - would work but Bun native is more idiomatic |\n| D4 | Error Line Number Extraction | YAMLException.mark property | js-yaml's YAMLException includes a 'mark' object with line/column when parsing fails. Standard approach for this library. | Regex parsing of error message - fragile and unnecessary |\n| D5 | Exit Code Priority | FILE_ERROR (2) > VALIDATION_ERROR (1) > SUCCESS (0) | Per requirements, file access errors take precedence. Users should fix file access issues before validation can proceed. | Equal priority - would lose diagnostic value |\n| D6 | Output Routing | Success to stdout, errors to stderr | Enables proper stream handling in pipelines. Success messages can be captured separately from errors. Standard Unix convention. | All to stdout - breaks pipeline conventions |\n| D7 | Batch Processing | Continue on errors, aggregate results | Per requirements REQ-002. Users need to see all issues at once, not just the first failure. | Fail-fast - rejected by requirements |\n| D8 | Summary Display | Show only for multiple files | Single-file validation needs no summary. Summary adds value when processing batches to quickly assess overall results. | Always show summary - verbose for single file case |\n| D9 | CLI Argument Parsing | Direct process.argv.slice(2) | Simple requirement (just file paths). No flags or options specified in requirements. Matches existing project pattern in src/index.ts. | yargs/commander - would add dependencies |\n| D10 | Test Framework | bun:test | Existing project test framework. No new dependencies. Consistent with existing test patterns in utils.test.ts. | Jest - would add dependency |\n\n## Detailed Rationales\n\n### D1: YAML Parser Library\n\nThe critical constraint of no new dependencies limits options significantly. Investigation of Bun runtime confirms that js-yaml is available as a built-in module. This library:\n\n- Is mature and widely used\n- Supports YAML 1.1 and 1.2 specifications\n- Provides detailed error information including line/column numbers\n- Has safe parsing mode by default (no code execution)\n\n### D2: Module Structure\n\nThe existing codebase demonstrates a pattern of separating concerns (utils.ts contains reusable functions, index.ts is the entry point). Following this pattern:\n\n- `types.ts` - Type definitions, no runtime code\n- `validator.ts` - Core validation logic, testable in isolation\n- `formatter.ts` - Output formatting, testable in isolation\n- `index.ts` - CLI wiring, integration point\n\nThis structure allows unit tests to focus on specific components without complex mocking.\n\n### D5: Exit Code Priority\n\nThe requirements specify three exit codes:\n- 0: All files valid\n- 1: One or more validation errors\n- 2: One or more file access errors\n\nBusiness rule BR-001 states file access errors take precedence because validation cannot be performed on inaccessible files. The implementation checks for file errors first when determining final exit code.\n\n### D6: Output Routing\n\nUnix convention and requirement clarity:\n- REQ-003: Error messages to stderr\n- REQ-004: Success messages to stdout\n\nThis separation enables:\n```bash\n# Capture only errors\nbun run validate *.yaml 2> errors.log\n\n# Capture only successes\nbun run validate *.yaml 1> valid-files.txt\n\n# Pipeline usage\nbun run validate *.yaml 2>&1 | grep \"line\"\n```\n\n### D9: CLI Argument Parsing\n\nThe requirements explicitly state:\n- File paths as positional arguments\n- No flags or options mentioned\n- Out of scope: stdin support, glob expansion, recursive scanning\n\nGiven these constraints, process.argv.slice(2) is sufficient and introduces no dependencies. If future requirements add flags, this decision should be revisited.\n"
