        "content": "# Requirements Specification: YAML Validator Command\n\n**Feature ID**: yaml-validator\n**Version**: 1.0.0\n**Status**: Draft\n**Created**: 2026-01-25\n\n## 1. Feature Overview\n\nA command-line utility that validates YAML file syntax, providing clear pass/fail feedback suitable for both interactive developer use and automated CI/CD pipeline integration. The validator must operate without introducing new dependencies to the project.\n\n## 2. Business Context\n\n### 2.1 Problem Statement\n\nDevelopers and CI/CD pipelines need a reliable way to validate YAML configuration files for syntax correctness before deployment or further processing. Invalid YAML files can cause runtime failures, deployment issues, and debugging overhead. Currently, there is no built-in validation command available in the project.\n\n### 2.2 Business Value\n\n- **Reduced debugging time**: Catch YAML syntax errors early in the development cycle\n- **Improved CI/CD reliability**: Prevent invalid configurations from progressing through pipelines\n- **Developer productivity**: Quick feedback loop for configuration file changes\n- **Zero additional overhead**: No new dependencies means no added maintenance burden or security surface\n\n### 2.3 Success Metrics\n\n| Metric | Target |\n|--------|--------|\n| Syntax error detection rate | 100% of invalid YAML syntax detected |\n| False positive rate | 0% (valid YAML never flagged as invalid) |\n| Command execution time | Under 1 second for typical configuration files |\n| CI/CD integration success | Exit codes correctly interpreted by common CI systems |\n\n## 3. Stakeholders & Users\n\n### 3.1 User Types\n\n| User Type | Description | Primary Need |\n|-----------|-------------|--------------|\n| Developer | Software engineers working with YAML configuration files | Quick validation during development |\n| CI/CD Pipeline | Automated build and deployment systems | Reliable validation with machine-readable exit codes |\n| DevOps Engineer | Personnel maintaining infrastructure configurations | Batch validation of multiple YAML files |\n\n### 3.2 Stakeholder Interests\n\n| Stakeholder | Interest |\n|-------------|----------|\n| Development Team | Consistent tooling without dependency bloat |\n| Operations Team | Reliable validation for infrastructure-as-code files |\n| Security Team | No new dependencies means reduced attack surface |\n\n## 4. Scope Definition\n\n### 4.1 In Scope\n\n- Command-line interface accepting one or more file paths as arguments\n- YAML syntax validation (structural correctness)\n- Human-readable error messages output to stderr\n- Pass/fail reporting for each file\n- Appropriate exit codes for different error conditions\n- Support for validating multiple files in a single invocation\n- Continuation through all files when errors are encountered (batch mode)\n\n### 4.2 Out of Scope\n\n- YAML schema validation (checking against a specific schema)\n- YAML semantic validation (checking values make sense for a specific application)\n- YAML file formatting or auto-correction\n- Interactive prompts or guided error resolution\n- YAML file editing capabilities\n- Support for reading from stdin (file paths only)\n- Recursive directory scanning\n- Glob pattern expansion (shell handles this)\n- Adding new project dependencies\n\n### 4.3 Assumptions\n\n- The existing project runtime/language has built-in YAML parsing capabilities\n- Users have filesystem access to the YAML files being validated\n- YAML files use standard YAML 1.1 or 1.2 syntax\n- File paths are provided explicitly (no implicit file discovery)\n\n## 5. Functional Requirements\n\n### REQ-001: Single File Validation\n**Priority**: Must Have\n**User Type**: Developer, CI/CD Pipeline\n**Requirement**: The validator shall accept a single file path as a command-line argument and validate its YAML syntax.\n**Rationale**: Single file validation is the most common use case for developers checking individual configuration files.\n**Acceptance Criteria**:\n- Given a valid file path to a syntactically correct YAML file, the command exits with code 0\n- Given a valid file path to a syntactically incorrect YAML file, the command exits with code 1\n- The file path is provided as a positional command-line argument\n\n### REQ-002: Multiple File Validation\n**Priority**: Must Have\n**User Type**: Developer, DevOps Engineer, CI/CD Pipeline\n**Requirement**: The validator shall accept multiple file paths as command-line arguments and validate each file's YAML syntax.\n**Rationale**: Batch validation improves efficiency when checking multiple configuration files.\n**Acceptance Criteria**:\n- Given multiple file paths, all files are validated regardless of individual failures\n- Validation continues through all files even when errors are encountered\n- Final exit code reflects the aggregate result of all validations\n\n### REQ-003: Syntax Error Reporting\n**Priority**: Must Have\n**User Type**: Developer\n**Requirement**: When a YAML syntax error is detected, the validator shall output a human-readable error message to stderr that includes the file name, line number (when available), and error description.\n**Rationale**: Developers need actionable information to locate and fix syntax errors quickly.\n**Acceptance Criteria**:\n- Error messages are written to stderr (not stdout)\n- Error messages include the file path that failed\n- Error messages include the line number where the error was detected (when available from parser)\n- Error messages include a description of what syntax error was found\n- Error messages are understandable without consulting external documentation\n\n### REQ-004: Success Reporting\n**Priority**: Should Have\n**User Type**: Developer\n**Requirement**: When a file passes validation, the validator shall output a brief success message to stdout.\n**Rationale**: Positive feedback confirms the validation ran and the file is valid.\n**Acceptance Criteria**:\n- Success messages are written to stdout\n- Success messages include the file path that was validated\n- Success messages are concise (single line per file)\n\n### REQ-005: Exit Code for Validation Success\n**Priority**: Must Have\n**User Type**: CI/CD Pipeline\n**Requirement**: The validator shall exit with code 0 when all provided files pass YAML syntax validation.\n**Rationale**: CI/CD systems interpret exit code 0 as success, allowing pipelines to proceed.\n**Acceptance Criteria**:\n- Exit code is 0 when all files are valid YAML\n- Exit code is 0 when a single valid file is provided\n- Exit code is 0 when multiple valid files are provided\n\n### REQ-006: Exit Code for Validation Errors\n**Priority**: Must Have\n**User Type**: CI/CD Pipeline\n**Requirement**: The validator shall exit with code 1 when one or more files fail YAML syntax validation.\n**Rationale**: Distinct exit code enables CI/CD systems to differentiate validation failures from other errors.\n**Acceptance Criteria**:\n- Exit code is 1 when any file contains invalid YAML syntax\n- Exit code is 1 regardless of how many files fail (one or multiple)\n- Exit code is 1 even if some files pass and others fail\n\n### REQ-007: Exit Code for File Access Errors\n**Priority**: Must Have\n**User Type**: CI/CD Pipeline\n**Requirement**: The validator shall exit with code 2 when one or more files cannot be found or read.\n**Rationale**: Distinguishing file access errors from validation errors helps users diagnose problems correctly.\n**Acceptance Criteria**:\n- Exit code is 2 when a specified file does not exist\n- Exit code is 2 when a specified file cannot be read (permission denied)\n- Exit code is 2 takes precedence if both file access errors and validation errors occur\n- Error message to stderr indicates which file(s) could not be accessed\n\n### REQ-008: No New Dependencies\n**Priority**: Must Have\n**User Type**: Development Team\n**Requirement**: The validator shall be implemented using only existing project dependencies and built-in language capabilities.\n**Rationale**: Minimizing dependencies reduces maintenance burden, security surface, and keeps the project lean.\n**Acceptance Criteria**:\n- No new entries added to dependency manifest files\n- Implementation uses existing YAML parsing capabilities\n- Feature functions correctly with current project dependencies\n\n### REQ-009: Aggregate Error Summary\n**Priority**: Should Have\n**User Type**: Developer, CI/CD Pipeline\n**Requirement**: When validating multiple files, the validator shall provide a summary of results after processing all files.\n**Rationale**: A summary helps users quickly understand the overall validation outcome.\n**Acceptance Criteria**:\n- Summary indicates total number of files checked\n- Summary indicates number of files that passed\n- Summary indicates number of files that failed\n- Summary is displayed after all individual file results\n\n## 6. Non-Functional Requirements\n\n### 6.1 Performance Expectations\n\n| Expectation | Target |\n|-------------|--------|\n| Single file validation | Under 500ms for files up to 10,000 lines |\n| Multiple file validation | Linear scaling with number of files |\n| Memory usage | Proportional to largest single file (not cumulative) |\n\n### 6.2 Security Requirements\n\n| Requirement | Description |\n|-------------|-------------|\n| No code execution | YAML parsing must not execute embedded code or scripts |\n| Path traversal prevention | File paths should be validated as regular files |\n| No network access | Validation operates entirely locally |\n\n### 6.3 Usability Requirements\n\n| Requirement | Description |\n|-------------|-------------|\n| Clear error messages | Errors should be actionable without external documentation |\n| Consistent output format | Output format should be predictable for parsing |\n| No interactive prompts | Command operates non-interactively for CI/CD compatibility |\n\n### 6.4 Compliance Requirements\n\nNo specific compliance requirements identified for this feature.\n\n## 7. User Stories\n\n### STORY-001: Developer Validates Single Configuration File\n**As a** developer\n**I want** to validate a YAML configuration file from the command line\n**So that** I can catch syntax errors before committing changes\n\n**Acceptance Criteria**:\n- GIVEN a developer has a YAML file at path `config.yaml`\n- WHEN they run the validator with `config.yaml` as an argument\n- THEN they see either a success message or a detailed error message\n- AND the exit code reflects the validation result\n\n### STORY-002: CI Pipeline Validates Multiple Config Files\n**As a** CI/CD pipeline\n**I want** to validate all YAML configuration files in a single command\n**So that** I can fail the build if any configuration is invalid\n\n**Acceptance Criteria**:\n- GIVEN a CI pipeline has multiple YAML files to validate\n- WHEN the validator is invoked with all file paths\n- THEN all files are validated regardless of individual failures\n- AND the exit code is non-zero if any file is invalid\n- AND the pipeline can parse the exit code to determine success or failure\n\n### STORY-003: DevOps Engineer Debugs Invalid YAML\n**As a** DevOps engineer\n**I want** to see the specific line and error when YAML validation fails\n**So that** I can quickly locate and fix the problem\n\n**Acceptance Criteria**:\n- GIVEN a YAML file with a syntax error on line 42\n- WHEN the validator processes the file\n- THEN the error message includes \"line 42\" or equivalent\n- AND the error message describes what syntax rule was violated\n- AND the error is written to stderr for proper stream handling\n\n### STORY-004: Developer Handles Missing File Gracefully\n**As a** developer\n**I want** clear feedback when I specify a non-existent file\n**So that** I understand the file path is wrong rather than the YAML being invalid\n\n**Acceptance Criteria**:\n- GIVEN a file path that does not exist\n- WHEN the validator attempts to process it\n- THEN an error message indicates the file was not found\n- AND the exit code is 2 (distinct from validation failure)\n\n## 8. Business Rules\n\n| Rule ID | Rule | Rationale |\n|---------|------|-----------|\n| BR-001 | File access errors take precedence over validation errors in exit codes | Users should address file access issues first as validation cannot proceed without readable files |\n| BR-002 | All files must be processed before returning final exit code | Batch operations should report all issues, not just the first one |\n| BR-003 | Empty files are considered valid YAML | An empty document is valid per YAML specification |\n| BR-004 | Files with only comments are considered valid YAML | Comment-only documents are valid per YAML specification |\n\n## 9. Dependencies & Constraints\n\n### Dependencies\n\n| Dependency | Type | Description |\n|------------|------|-------------|\n| Existing YAML parser | Internal | Project must have existing YAML parsing capability |\n| Filesystem access | System | Standard file read operations |\n\n### Constraints\n\n| Constraint | Description |\n|------------|-------------|\n| No new dependencies | Implementation limited to existing project capabilities |\n| CLI interface only | No GUI or web interface required |\n| Local files only | No remote file fetching or URL support |\n\n## 10. Clarifications Log\n\n| Date | Question | Clarification | Source |\n|------|----------|---------------|--------|\n| 2026-01-25 | What is the input format? | File path(s) as command-line arguments (one or more files) | User clarification |\n| 2026-01-25 | What type of validation? | Syntax only (check if valid YAML) | User clarification |\n| 2026-01-25 | What is the output format? | Simple pass/fail with human-readable error messages to stderr | User clarification |\n| 2026-01-25 | Can new dependencies be added? | No new dependencies allowed | User clarification |\n| 2026-01-25 | How to handle multiple file failures? | Continue and report all errors at end | User clarification |\n| 2026-01-25 | What exit codes to use? | 0=success, 1=validation errors, 2=file not found/read errors | User clarification |\n| 2026-01-25 | Who are the target users? | Developers and CI/CD pipelines | User clarification |\n"
