        "content": "# Requirements Specification: YAML Validator Command\n\n**Feature ID**: yaml-validator-command\n**Version**: 1.0.0\n**Status**: Draft\n**Created**: 2026-01-25\n\n## 1. Feature Overview\n\nA command-line utility that validates YAML files for syntax correctness, providing clear feedback on valid files and detailed error information for invalid files. The validator must be implemented using only existing project dependencies to maintain a minimal footprint.\n\n## 2. Business Context\n\n### 2.1 Problem Statement\n\nUsers working with YAML configuration files need a quick way to verify that their files are syntactically correct before using them in downstream processes. Currently, there is no built-in mechanism in the project to validate YAML files, requiring users to use external tools or wait for runtime errors.\n\n### 2.2 Business Value\n\n- Reduces time spent debugging configuration issues by catching YAML syntax errors early\n- Improves developer productivity by providing immediate validation feedback\n- Maintains project simplicity by avoiding additional dependencies\n- Supports DevOps workflows where YAML configuration validation is critical\n\n### 2.3 Success Metrics\n\n| Metric | Target |\n|--------|--------|\n| Validation accuracy | 100% of valid YAML files pass; 100% of invalid YAML files fail with error details |\n| Response time | Validation completes within 500ms for files under 1MB |\n| Error clarity | Error messages include line number and description of the issue |\n\n## 3. Stakeholders & Users\n\n### 3.1 User Types\n\n| User Type | Description | Primary Needs |\n|-----------|-------------|---------------|\n| Developer | Team members creating or modifying YAML configuration files | Quick syntax validation during development |\n| DevOps Engineer | Personnel managing CI/CD pipelines and deployment configurations | Validate YAML files as part of automated workflows |\n| End User | Anyone using CLI tools in the project | Simple command to check YAML file validity |\n\n### 3.2 Stakeholder Interests\n\n| Stakeholder | Interest |\n|-------------|----------|\n| Project Maintainers | Keep dependency footprint minimal; maintain code simplicity |\n| Development Team | Reliable validation with clear error messages |\n| Operations | Integration capability with scripts and CI pipelines |\n\n## 4. Scope Definition\n\n### 4.1 In Scope\n\n- Command-line interface for validating YAML files\n- Validation of YAML syntax correctness\n- Clear success/failure output messages\n- Error reporting with line numbers when available\n- Support for validating single files via file path argument\n- Support for validating YAML content from standard input (stdin)\n- Exit codes suitable for scripting (0 for success, non-zero for failure)\n\n### 4.2 Out of Scope\n\n- YAML schema validation (JSON Schema, custom schemas)\n- YAML file formatting or pretty-printing\n- Batch validation of multiple files in a single command\n- YAML-to-JSON conversion or vice versa\n- Validation against application-specific rules\n- GUI or web interface\n- Adding new npm dependencies\n\n### 4.3 Assumptions\n\n- Bun runtime provides sufficient YAML parsing capabilities or the implementation can use a pure JavaScript/TypeScript parser\n- Users have access to a terminal/command-line environment\n- YAML files are encoded in UTF-8\n\n## 5. Functional Requirements\n\n### FR-001: File Path Validation\n- **Priority**: Must Have\n- **User Type**: All users\n- **Requirement**: The system shall accept a file path as a command-line argument and validate the YAML content of that file\n- **Rationale**: Users need to validate specific YAML files by providing their location\n- **Acceptance Criteria**:\n  - GIVEN a valid file path to a YAML file\n  - WHEN the user runs the validator command with that path\n  - THEN the system reads the file and reports whether it is valid YAML\n\n### FR-002: Standard Input Validation\n- **Priority**: Should Have\n- **User Type**: DevOps Engineer, Developer\n- **Requirement**: The system shall accept YAML content from standard input for validation when no file path is provided\n- **Rationale**: Enables piping content from other commands and integration with scripts\n- **Acceptance Criteria**:\n  - GIVEN YAML content piped to stdin\n  - WHEN the user runs the validator command without a file argument\n  - THEN the system validates the piped content\n\n### FR-003: Success Output\n- **Priority**: Must Have\n- **User Type**: All users\n- **Requirement**: The system shall display a clear success message when the YAML is valid\n- **Rationale**: Users need confirmation that their file passed validation\n- **Acceptance Criteria**:\n  - GIVEN a valid YAML file or content\n  - WHEN validation completes successfully\n  - THEN the system outputs a success message (e.g., \"Valid YAML\")\n  - AND returns exit code 0\n\n### FR-004: Error Output with Details\n- **Priority**: Must Have\n- **User Type**: All users\n- **Requirement**: The system shall display detailed error information when YAML is invalid, including line number and error description when available\n- **Rationale**: Users need actionable information to fix syntax errors\n- **Acceptance Criteria**:\n  - GIVEN an invalid YAML file or content\n  - WHEN validation fails\n  - THEN the system outputs an error message with line number (if available) and error description\n  - AND returns a non-zero exit code\n\n### FR-005: File Not Found Handling\n- **Priority**: Must Have\n- **User Type**: All users\n- **Requirement**: The system shall display an appropriate error message when the specified file does not exist\n- **Rationale**: Users need clear feedback when they provide an incorrect file path\n- **Acceptance Criteria**:\n  - GIVEN a file path that does not exist\n  - WHEN the user runs the validator command\n  - THEN the system outputs \"File not found: [path]\" or similar message\n  - AND returns a non-zero exit code\n\n### FR-006: Help Information\n- **Priority**: Should Have\n- **User Type**: All users\n- **Requirement**: The system shall display usage information when invoked with a help flag (--help or -h)\n- **Rationale**: Users need guidance on how to use the command\n- **Acceptance Criteria**:\n  - GIVEN the user runs the command with --help or -h flag\n  - WHEN the command executes\n  - THEN usage information is displayed including available options and examples\n\n## 6. Non-Functional Requirements\n\n### 6.1 Performance Expectations\n\n| Requirement | Target |\n|-------------|--------|\n| Validation time for files under 1MB | Less than 500ms |\n| Memory usage | Should not exceed 50MB for files under 1MB |\n| Startup time | Command should start within 200ms |\n\n### 6.2 Security Requirements\n\n| Requirement | Description |\n|-------------|-------------|\n| File access | Only read access to specified files; no write operations |\n| Path traversal | Validate file paths to prevent unintended file access (informational only; rely on OS permissions) |\n| No code execution | YAML content must not be executed; validation only |\n\n### 6.3 Usability Requirements\n\n| Requirement | Description |\n|-------------|-------------|\n| Clear messaging | All output messages should be human-readable and actionable |\n| Consistent exit codes | Exit code 0 for success, non-zero for all error conditions |\n| No color dependency | Output should be readable without color support (plain text fallback) |\n\n### 6.4 Compliance Requirements\n\n| Requirement | Description |\n|-------------|-------------|\n| YAML 1.2 support | Should validate against YAML 1.2 specification where possible |\n\n## 7. User Stories\n\n### STORY-001: Validate Configuration File\n- **As a** Developer\n- **I want** to validate my YAML configuration file from the command line\n- **So that** I can catch syntax errors before deploying my application\n\n**Acceptance Criteria**:\n- GIVEN I have a YAML file at path `./config.yaml`\n- WHEN I run `bun run src/yaml-validate.ts ./config.yaml`\n- THEN I see \"Valid YAML\" if the file is correct\n- OR I see an error message with line number if there is a syntax error\n\n### STORY-002: Validate Piped Content\n- **As a** DevOps Engineer\n- **I want** to pipe YAML content into the validator\n- **So that** I can integrate validation into my shell scripts and CI pipelines\n\n**Acceptance Criteria**:\n- GIVEN I have YAML content in a variable or from another command\n- WHEN I run `echo \"$YAML_CONTENT\" | bun run src/yaml-validate.ts`\n- THEN I receive validation results for the piped content\n\n### STORY-003: Quick Syntax Check\n- **As a** End User\n- **I want** a simple command to check if my YAML file is valid\n- **So that** I do not need to install additional tools\n\n**Acceptance Criteria**:\n- GIVEN I am in the project directory\n- WHEN I run the validation command with my file\n- THEN I get immediate feedback on validity\n- AND the command works without installing new dependencies\n\n## 8. Business Rules\n\n| Rule ID | Rule |\n|---------|------|\n| BR-001 | No new npm dependencies may be added to implement this feature |\n| BR-002 | Empty files should be considered valid (empty YAML document) |\n| BR-003 | Files with only comments should be considered valid |\n| BR-004 | Multi-document YAML files (with `---` separators) should be validated as a whole |\n\n## 9. Dependencies & Constraints\n\n| Type | Description |\n|------|-------------|\n| Technical Constraint | Must use only existing dependencies (Bun runtime and @types/bun) |\n| Technical Constraint | Must work with Bun runtime (not Node.js specific APIs unless compatible) |\n| Dependency | Relies on Bun's built-in YAML parsing capabilities or pure JS implementation |\n\n## 10. Clarifications Log\n\n| Question | Resolution | Source |\n|----------|------------|--------|\n| What YAML features must be supported? | Standard YAML 1.2 syntax validation | Conservative default |\n| Should the validator support multiple files? | No, single file or stdin only for MVP | Scope constraint - \"simple\" requirement |\n| What about schema validation? | Out of scope - syntax validation only | Conservative default |\n\n---\n\n## AFK Mode: Auto-Selected Defaults\n\n| Decision Point | Choice | Rationale |\n|----------------|--------|-----------|\n| PRD selection | None available | No PRDs found in .rp1/work/prds/ |\n| Feature scope | Single file + stdin validation | Requirement specified \"simple\" - conservative MVP scope |\n| YAML version | YAML 1.2 | Industry standard; conservative default |\n| Multi-document support | Validate as whole | Standard YAML behavior |\n\n## AFK Mode: Inferred Decisions\n\n| Ambiguity | Resolution | Source |\n|-----------|------------|--------|\n| \"simple\" validator definition | Syntax validation only, no schema support | Conservative interpretation of requirement |\n| \"without adding new deps\" | Use Bun built-in capabilities or pure TS implementation | Direct from requirement; Bun has js-yaml compatible parsing |\n| Command interface | CLI with file path argument and stdin support | Inferred from project pattern (src/index.ts uses process.argv) |\n| Output format | Plain text messages with exit codes | Standard CLI convention |\n| What constitutes valid YAML | Parseable without syntax errors per YAML 1.2 spec | Conservative default |\n"
      },
      "source": "assistant"
