        "content": "# Design Decisions: YAML Validator Command\n\n**Feature ID**: yaml-validator-command\n**Created**: 2026-01-25\n\n## Decision Log\n\n| ID | Decision | Choice | Rationale | Alternatives Considered |\n|----|----------|--------|-----------|------------------------|\n| D1 | YAML parsing approach | Pure TypeScript syntax validator | Constraint BR-001 prohibits new npm dependencies; full YAML parser (js-yaml, yaml) would require adding deps; Bun has no built-in YAML parser | 1. Add js-yaml (violates constraint) 2. Bundled YAML parser (large, complex) 3. JSON.parse for subset (too limited) |\n| D2 | File organization | Separate validator module (yaml-validator.ts) + CLI entry (yaml-validate.ts) | Separation of concerns; testability; follows existing pattern of utils.ts separation | 1. Single file (harder to test) 2. Add to utils.ts (unrelated functionality) |\n| D3 | Exit code scheme | 0=valid, 1=invalid, 2=not found, 3=read error | Standard Unix CLI conventions; enables scripting with specific error handling | 1. Only 0/1 (less granular) 2. Negative codes (non-standard) |\n| D4 | Error message format | \"Error at line N: description\" | Matches FR-004 requirement for line numbers; IDE/editor compatible format | 1. JSON output (less human-readable) 2. No line numbers (violates requirement) |\n| D5 | stdin detection | Absence of file argument | Simple logic; follows common CLI patterns; existing project uses process.argv | 1. Explicit --stdin flag (more verbose) 2. Auto-detect TTY (complex) |\n| D6 | Test framework | bun:test | Already used in project (utils.test.ts); no additional dependencies | N/A - only option given constraints |\n| D7 | Indentation validation | Spaces only, reject tabs | YAML 1.2 spec recommends spaces; tabs cause common errors | 1. Allow tabs (against spec) 2. Auto-convert tabs (out of scope) |\n| D8 | Empty file handling | Valid (empty document) | Per BR-002; valid YAML per spec | 1. Invalid (stricter but non-compliant) |\n\n## AFK Mode: Auto-Selected Technology Decisions\n\n| Decision | Choice | Source | Rationale |\n|----------|--------|--------|-----------|\n| Runtime | Bun | Codebase | Project uses Bun exclusively (package.json scripts, bun:test) |\n| Language | TypeScript | Codebase | All source files are .ts; tsconfig.json present |\n| Testing | bun:test | Codebase | Existing tests use bun:test (utils.test.ts) |\n| File I/O | Bun.file() | Codebase analysis | Standard Bun API for file operations |\n| Stdin reading | Bun.stdin | Codebase analysis | Standard Bun API for stdin |\n| Module pattern | Named exports | Codebase | utils.ts uses named exports |\n| Test location | Co-located *.test.ts | Codebase | utils.test.ts pattern |\n| Documentation style | JSDoc comments | Codebase | Existing files use JSDoc |\n\n## Technical Tradeoffs\n\n### D1: Pure TypeScript Validator\n\n**Tradeoff**: Completeness vs. Dependencies\n\n- **Chose**: Partial YAML validation without full parser\n- **Gave up**: Full YAML 1.2 compliance for all edge cases\n- **Why acceptable**: Most real-world YAML errors are caught by basic syntax checks (quotes, indentation, brackets). The constraint of no new dependencies is a hard requirement.\n\n### D3: Granular Exit Codes\n\n**Tradeoff**: Simplicity vs. Scriptability\n\n- **Chose**: Multiple specific exit codes\n- **Gave up**: Simpler 0/1 binary result\n- **Why acceptable**: Enables better CI/CD integration; scripts can distinguish between \"invalid YAML\" and \"file not found\"\n\n## Assumptions Made\n\n| Assumption | Confidence | Impact if Wrong |\n|------------|------------|-----------------|\n| Bun.file() and Bun.stdin APIs are stable | HIGH | LOW - well-documented Bun features |\n| Basic syntax validator catches most common errors | MEDIUM | HIGH - core functionality |\n| UTF-8 encoding for all input files | HIGH | LOW - standard for YAML |\n| Users have terminal access | HIGH | LOW - stated in requirements |\n"
      },
